Q: SVG circle appears briefly at (0,0) before animating to correct position — how to fix? A: <p><strong>TL;DR:</strong> The flicker wasn't caused by the pulsating dot I originally asked about — it was caused by <em>other</em> circles in the same SVG that used <code>&lt;animateMotion&gt;</code> without explicit <code>cx</code>/<code>cy</code> values. The fix requires <code>cx=&quot;0&quot; cy=&quot;0&quot;</code> plus <code>visibility=&quot;hidden&quot;</code> with a delayed <code>&lt;animate fill=&quot;freeze&quot;&gt;</code> to make them visible.</p>
<p>My fail: I was looking in the wrong place. Originally I focused on a single pulsating circle at <code>cx=&quot;316&quot; cy=&quot;59.5&quot;</code>. But the actual SVG contained <strong>two additional circles</strong> using <code>&lt;animateMotion&gt;</code> to travel along motion paths. Those circles had no <code>cy</code> attribute set, which defaults to <code>0</code>. So on hard reload — before SMIL kicked in — they rendered briefly at <code>(cx, 0)</code>, which visually corresponds to the top edge of the SVG and, depending on layout, the upper-left corner of the browser viewport.</p>
<p>The pulsating circle was a red herring. Hiding only that one had no effect because the flash was coming from the motion-path circles.</p>
<p>There were two interacting problems:</p>
<ol>
<li><p><strong>Default position of <code>animateMotion</code> circles</strong></p>
<p><code>&lt;animateMotion&gt;</code> works by applying a translation transform relative to the element's origin. To make the motion path render at absolute coordinates, the circle should sit at <code>cx=&quot;0&quot; cy=&quot;0&quot;</code>. But that means without intervention, the circle is initially visible at the SVG origin (top-left) before SMIL starts.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong><code>&lt;set&gt;</code> doesn't survive <code>repeatCount=&quot;indefinite&quot;</code></strong></p>
<p>Using <code>&lt;set attributeName=&quot;visibility&quot; to=&quot;visible&quot; begin=&quot;0.1s&quot; /&gt;</code> works initially, but in some browsers the visibility state gets reset between animation iterations of repeating animations on the same element, causing the dot to disappear and reappear mid-loop.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p>To solve the problem I tried unsuccessfully:</p>
<ul>
<li><p><code>opacity=&quot;0&quot;</code> on the circle (filters create separate composite layers that can leak through)</p>
</li>
<li><p><code>visibility: hidden</code> on the parent container in CSS (SVG children don't inherit <code>visibility</code> from parents — the child needs explicit <code>visibility=&quot;inherit&quot;</code> or its own hidden state)</p>
</li>
<li><p><code>opacity: 0</code> with fade-in on the wrapping div (filters bypass parent opacity in some browsers during initial paint)</p>
</li>
<li><p>Toggling visibility via Angular <code>AfterViewInit</code> / <code>requestAnimationFrame</code> / <code>setTimeout</code> (too late — the flash happens before Angular's lifecycle hooks fire)</p>
</li>
<li><p><code>&lt;set&gt;</code> element for visibility (gets lost on repeat)</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>What finally worked:</p>
<p>For each <code>animateMotion</code> circle: set <code>cx=&quot;0&quot; cy=&quot;0&quot;</code>, add <code>visibility=&quot;hidden&quot;</code>, and use <code>&lt;animate ... fill=&quot;freeze&quot;&gt;</code> instead of <code>&lt;set&gt;</code> to make it visible after a short delay. The delay needs to be ≥ the time the browser needs to settle initial layout (100ms is enough).</p>
<pre><code>&lt;!-- Circle traveling along a motion path --&gt;
&lt;circle cx=&quot;0&quot; cy=&quot;0&quot; r=&quot;7&quot; fill=&quot;url(#myGradient)&quot; filter=&quot;url(#glow)&quot;
        opacity=&quot;0&quot; visibility=&quot;hidden&quot;&gt;
  &lt;animate attributeName=&quot;visibility&quot; to=&quot;visible&quot;
           begin=&quot;0.1s&quot; dur=&quot;1s&quot; fill=&quot;freeze&quot; /&gt;
  &lt;animateMotion
    dur=&quot;2.2s&quot;
    begin=&quot;0.1s&quot;
    repeatCount=&quot;indefinite&quot;
    calcMode=&quot;spline&quot;
    keyPoints=&quot;0;1&quot;
    keyTimes=&quot;0;1&quot;
    keySplines=&quot;0.2 0 0.8 1&quot;&gt;
    &lt;mpath href=&quot;#myPath&quot; /&gt;
  &lt;/animateMotion&gt;
  &lt;animate attributeName=&quot;opacity&quot;
           dur=&quot;2.2s&quot; begin=&quot;0.1s&quot; repeatCount=&quot;indefinite&quot;
           values=&quot;0;1;1;0&quot; keyTimes=&quot;0;0.1;0.85;1&quot; /&gt;
&lt;/circle&gt;

&lt;!-- Stationary pulsating circle --&gt;
&lt;circle cx=&quot;316&quot; cy=&quot;59.5&quot; r=&quot;7&quot; fill=&quot;url(#myGradient)&quot; filter=&quot;url(#glow)&quot;
        visibility=&quot;hidden&quot;&gt;
  &lt;animate attributeName=&quot;visibility&quot; to=&quot;visible&quot;
           begin=&quot;0.1s&quot; dur=&quot;1s&quot; fill=&quot;freeze&quot; /&gt;
  &lt;animate attributeName=&quot;r&quot;
           dur=&quot;1.5s&quot; begin=&quot;0.1s&quot; repeatCount=&quot;indefinite&quot;
           values=&quot;7;11;7&quot; keyTimes=&quot;0;0.5;1&quot;
           calcMode=&quot;spline&quot; keySplines=&quot;0.4 0 0.6 1; 0.4 0 0.6 1&quot; /&gt;
&lt;/circle&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Cheatsheet:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>For <code>&lt;animateMotion&gt;</code> circles, <code>cx=&quot;0&quot; cy=&quot;0&quot;</code> is the correct origin — but you must hide them initially or they'll flash at the SVG origin</p>
</li>
<li><p>Use <code>&lt;animate ... fill=&quot;freeze&quot;&gt;</code> instead of <code>&lt;set&gt;</code> for visibility on elements with repeating animations</p>
</li>
<li><p>Synchronize all <code>begin</code> values across the visibility, motion, and opacity animations on the same element</p>
</li>
<li><p>When SVG flickers on hard reload, check <strong>every</strong> animated element for missing initial coordinates — not just the one that seems suspicious</p>
</li>
</ul>

Q: How to process massive CSV files in C without high RAM overhead? A: <p>One efficient way to handle massive CSV ingestion in C is by utilizing <strong>Memory Mapping (<code>mmap</code>)</strong>.</p>
<p>Instead of using <code>fread()</code> or <code>read()</code>, which involves copying data from the kernel buffer to a user-space buffer (the &quot;double-copy&quot; penalty), <code>mmap()</code> maps the file directly into the process's virtual address space.</p>
<p><strong>Technical Advantages:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><p><strong>Reduced I/O Overhead:</strong> By bypassing standard buffered I/O, you eliminate the overhead of explicit system calls for every block and the associated memory copies.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Hardware Pre-fetching:</strong> Sequential access to the mapped memory triggers the OS and CPU hardware pre-fetchers. This pulls data into the L1/L2 cache before the application loop even hits the next pointer address.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Efficiency:</strong> Memory is only paged into physical RAM as needed, keeping the heap footprint near zero even for multi-gigabyte files.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Implementation Example (C):</strong></p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;sys/mman.h&gt;
#include &lt;sys/stat.h&gt;
#include &lt;fcntl.h&gt;
#include &lt;unistd.h&gt;

// ... inside main or processing function ...

    int fd = open(filepath, O_RDONLY);
    if (fd == -1) return 1;

    struct stat sb;
    if (fstat(fd, &amp;sb) == -1) {
        close(fd);
        return 1;
    }

    // Map the file into memory
    char *addr = mmap(NULL, sb.st_size, PROT_READ, MAP_PRIVATE, fd, 0);
    if (addr == MAP_FAILED) {
        close(fd);
        return 1;
    }

    // Sequential scan for newline characters
    long long rows = 0;
    for (size_t i = 0; i &lt; sb.st_size; i++) {
        if (addr[i] == '\n') {
            rows++;
        }
    }

    // Essential Cleanup
    munmap(addr, sb.st_size);
    close(fd);
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Benchmark Observations:</strong> In tests on a 10M row CSV (1GB), this implementation consistently completes in approximately <strong>0.20s</strong> on standard NVMe hardware, compared to <strong>2.70s</strong> using Python/Pandas, with significantly lower peak memory usage.</p>
<p><strong>Disclosure:</strong> I have implemented this logic into a hardened, open-source tool called <strong>Axiom</strong> to handle edge cases like empty files and validation. Full source is available here: <a href="https://www.google.com/search?q=https://github.com/naresh-cn2/axiom-zero-ram" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://github.com/naresh-cn2/axiom-zero-ram</a></p>
